/*
 * Copyright (c) 2026, LexxPluss Inc.
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
 *
 * Reserving the top 4 KiB of DTCM for records that must survive a reset.
 *
 * WHY THIS FILE EXISTS RATHER THAN A COMMENT. The diagnostic images place their forensics blocks at
 * 0x2001F000 and 0x2001F200 on the strength of an observation -- that the application links nothing
 * into DTCM, so its .dtcm sections are empty in every image built so far. That is true and it is not
 * a reservation: the first translation unit to ask for DTCM would be placed there by a linker that
 * has never been told the region is spoken for, and nothing would fail to build.
 *
 * Shrinking the memory node is a reservation the toolchain enforces. The 4 KiB above 0x2001F000 is
 * no longer part of the region the linker allocates from, so no section can land in it, and the
 * failure mode if somebody later needs that space is an out-of-region link error rather than a
 * forensics record that silently stopped being one.
 *
 * WHAT LIVES THERE. 0x2001F000 the hang record and 0x2001F200 the I2C forensics block, both DEV
 * only; 0x2001FF00 the watchdog tombstone, 256 bytes, which is the only one a released image
 * carries. The tombstone's own header asserts that it clears the other two.
 *
 * COST. Nothing measurable today: DTCM holds no application data, so the region being 124 KiB
 * instead of 128 changes no placement. It is written down here so that a future change which does
 * want DTCM meets the reservation instead of the records.
 */

&dtcm {
	reg = <0x20000000 DT_SIZE_K(124)>;
};
